{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#3268","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#3268","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#3268\",\"parent_revision\":\"e1125ae4c19c7468173f1f46e001a9f0bb198329\",\"root_revision\":\"d1bffde53604e40fbc01e934f4db6be507df276763a9b7446feac66b1f07d43c\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: P2\\ntype: Bug\\nstatus: open\\nadded: 2026-08-28\\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\nObserved 2026-08-28 while running `/dh:work-backlog-item groom #3169`: dispatching the grooming-swarm agents surfaced two related problems in how the orchestrator is supposed to construct subagent prompts, and a third instance turned up in a different workflow while investigating.\\n\\n**Every other swarm agent's own file requires only `item_ref`/`selector` as input.** Checked directly: `impact-analyst.md`, `fact-checker.md`, `classifier.md`, and `alignment-analyst.md` each declare `## Input` as just `item_ref`/`selector`, and each has an explicit Phase 1 instruction to call `mcp__plugin_dh_backlog__backlog_view` itself to fetch the item. `backlog-item-groomer.md` is the one exception: its `## Input` section names literal content fields \u2014 item title, item description, research questions, RT-ICA summary \u2014 and the file contains zero `backlog_view` calls anywhere, even though its own frontmatter `tools:` list already includes `mcp__plugin_dh_backlog` (so lack of tool access is not the reason). The orchestrator-facing workflow doc (`skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/groom/swarm.md`, \\\"Groomer prompt\\\" section) reinforces this by listing an even longer set of content the orchestrator must paste in: title, description, source, priority, plan address, RT-ICA assessment, Fact-Check verdicts, Issue Classification, Root-Cause Analysis, Impact Radius, Research section. Every one of these already exists as a section on the backlog item, reachable by the same `backlog_view` call the other four agents already make themselves \u2014 the groomer's contract just doesn't ask it to. The same document's sequence diagram also nudges toward pasting content for the other four agents (\\\"spawn (item details, ...)\\\", \\\"spawn (item description)\\\") even though those agents' own files are unambiguous about self-fetching \u2014 looser wording, but a contributing cause of the same over-pasting.\\n\\n**Nothing in the swarm.md contract or the agent files constrains what else an orchestrator may write into a dispatch prompt beyond the required input.** During this same grooming run, the orchestrator (this agent) wrote a classifier dispatch prompt that stated its own conclusion \u2014 \\\"this is very likely `defect` ... confirm this classification yourself\\\" \u2014 before the classifier had done any independent reasoning. Nothing in `classifier.md` or `swarm.md` flags this as out of bounds; the only reason it didn't corrupt the result is that the classifier's own 5-whys procedure happened to produce independently-computed evidence (e.g. an exact `difflib.SequenceMatcher` ratio) rather than simply agreeing with the framing it was handed. For a differently-designed agent, or a less careful run, an orchestrator's speculative framing could reach an agent whose entire value is independent verification (fact-checker, classifier, alignment-analyst) and bias its output without any contract violation being detectable after the fact \u2014 the written section would look like an independent verdict either way.\\n\\n**The same pasted-content pattern recurs outside the groom workflow, with a generic executor instead of a specialized one.** `skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/close/start.md` Step 5.6 (~line 121) extracts `Acceptance Criteria` from `backlog_view` for the orchestrator's own parsing, then re-types item title, plan address, and each criterion individually into a `dh:task-worker` verification-agent's prompt. `task-worker` already has full `mcp__plugin_dh_backlog` access \u2014 nothing prevents telling it the `item_ref` and the section name and letting it call `backlog_view(selector=item_ref, section=\\\"Acceptance Criteria\\\")` itself, the same way the four self-fetching groom-swarm agents do.\\n\\nAll three instances share a root cause: there is no documented rule for what belongs in a dynamic subagent prompt versus what belongs in the agent's own file or a shared skill (e.g. `dh:subagent-contract`, which four of the five groom-swarm agents already load) versus what the orchestrator must never add (speculative conclusions, pre-formed verdicts). The groomer's contract inconsistency and the close-workflow verification-agent prompt are the concrete, checkable symptoms; the anchoring risk is the less visible consequence of the same missing rule.\\n\\n## RT-ICA\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-08-28T13:21:50.083980Z</sub>\\n\\nRT-ICA Snapshot: fix: Grooming-swarm subagent dispatch contract is inconsistent: one agent requires orchestrator-pasted content while the rest self-fetch, and nothing constrains what an orchestrator may add to a dispatch prompt\\nDate: 2026-08-28\\nGoal: Establish a documented rule for what belongs in a dynamic subagent dispatch prompt (item_ref/selector plus genuinely dynamic cross-agent findings) versus what belongs in the agent's own file or a shared skill, versus what the orchestrator must never add (speculative conclusions, pre-formed verdicts) \u2014 and bring the three found instances into line with it.\\nConditions:\\n1. `backlog-item-groomer.md`'s `## Input` section names literal content fields (title, description, research questions, RT-ICA summary) and contains zero `backlog_view` calls, despite having `mcp__plugin_dh_backlog` tool access. | Status: AVAILABLE\\n2. `impact-analyst.md`, `fact-checker.md`, `classifier.md`, `alignment-analyst.md` each declare `## Input` as only `item_ref`/`selector` and each has an explicit Phase 1 `backlog_view` self-fetch call. | Status: AVAILABLE\\n3. `swarm.md`'s \\\"Groomer prompt\\\" section explicitly lists a long set of content the orchestrator must paste for the groomer (title, description, source, priority, plan address, RT-ICA, Fact-Check, Issue Classification, Root-Cause Analysis, Impact Radius, Research) \u2014 all of which already exist as sections on the item. Its sequence diagram also uses looser \\\"item details\\\"/\\\"item description\\\" phrasing for the other four agents, contributing to over-pasting even though those agents' own files are unambiguous. | Status: AVAILABLE\\n4. The classifier-anchoring incident (orchestrator stated its own conclusion before the classifier reasoned) \u2014 happened this session, exact prompt text known, and nothing in `classifier.md` or `swarm.md` prohibits it. | Status: AVAILABLE\\n5. `close/start.md` Step 5.6 (~line 121) re-types item title, plan address, and each acceptance criterion into a `dh:task-worker` prompt instead of having it self-fetch via `item_ref` + section name, despite `task-worker` having full `mcp__plugin_dh_backlog` access. | Status: AVAILABLE\\n6. Content of `dh:subagent-contract`, the shared skill four of the five groom-swarm agents already load \u2014 not yet read. Directly relevant to where a fix should live (this skill may already partially address prompt discipline, or may be the natural home for a new rule). | Status: DERIVABLE\\n7. Whether the same pasted-content-instead-of-self-fetch pattern exists in other development-harness workflows beyond work-backlog-item (e.g. dh:add-new-feature/dh:implement-feature's specialist agent dispatches \u2014 plan-validator, contract-verification, feature-verifier, codebase-analyzer) \u2014 explicitly unaudited, noted as out of scope during creation. Affects how broad the Impact Radius/fix should be. | Status: DERIVABLE\\n8. `task-worker.md`'s own Identity describes a SAM-task-plan executor (reads via `sam_task`, not ad-hoc prompts) \u2014 the close/start.md and groom/error.md dispatches use it in a materially different, ad-hoc mode. Whether this is in-scope for this item's fix (vs. a separate identity-mismatch issue) is a scoping call, not yet made. | Status: DERIVABLE\\nAVAILABLE count: 5\\nDERIVABLE count: 3\\nMISSING count: 0\\n</div>\\n\\n## Impact Radius\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-08-28T13:43:10.695421Z</sub>\\n\\n**Core inconsistency (must change together):**\\n- `plugins/development-harness/agents/backlog-item-groomer.md` \u2014 `## Input` section names literal content fields instead of `item_ref`; has zero `backlog_view` calls despite `mcp__plugin_dh_backlog` tool access. Primary fix target: add Phase 1 self-fetch, rewrite `## Input` to match the other four agents.\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/groom/swarm.md` \u2014 \\\"Groomer prompt\\\" section (~line 255) lists the full paste-in field set (title, description, source, priority, plan address, RT-ICA, Fact-Check, Issue Classification, Root-Cause Analysis, Impact Radius, Research) that must shrink to `item_ref` once the groomer self-fetches. Sequence diagram lines 65/67/68 (\\\"spawn (item details, ...)\\\", \\\"spawn (item description)\\\") need tightening to match the self-fetch contract already followed by the other four agents.\\n\\n**Reference pattern (should not regress, verify unaffected):**\\n- `plugins/development-harness/agents/impact-analyst.md`, `fact-checker.md`, `classifier.md`, `alignment-analyst.md` \u2014 each already declares `## Input` as `item_ref`/`selector` only and self-fetches via `backlog_view` in Phase 1. These are the target shape; no functional change expected, but any new shared-skill rule (see below) must not conflict with their existing Phase 1 wording.\\n\\n**Anchoring-risk instance (same root cause, no file with a text fix \u2014 needs a documented constraint):**\\n- No single file currently prohibits an orchestrator from writing pre-formed conclusions into a dispatch prompt (the classifier-anchoring incident from this session). The natural home is `plugins/development-harness/skills/subagent-contract/` (SKILL.md \u2014 confirm exact filename before editing), since it is the shared skill already loaded by all five groom-swarm agents. Because 25 agent files under `plugins/development-harness/agents/` load `subagent-contract` (grep-confirmed: generic-stage-agent, reviewer-security, code-reviewer, t0-baseline-capture, rtica-assessor, backlog-item-groomer, doc-drift-auditor, contract-verification, codebase-analyzer, reviewer-accessibility, reviewer-performance, service-docs-maintainer, alignment-analyst, feature-researcher, review-synthesizer, impact-analyst, classifier, integration-checker, plan-validator, reviewer-quality, swarm-task-planner, ecosystem-researcher, feature-verifier, fact-checker, task-worker, context-refinement, tn-verification-gate, dh-context-gathering), any new rule added there has a wide blast radius: it constrains every orchestrator that dispatches these 25 agents, not just the groom swarm. This argues for additive, narrowly-scoped wording (a \\\"what the orchestrator must never add\\\" rule) rather than a rewrite of the shared skill.\\n\\n**Second instance, different workflow (close):**\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/close/start.md` (Step 5.6, ~line 121) \u2014 re-types item title, plan address, and each acceptance criterion individually into a `dh:task-worker` prompt instead of passing `item_ref` + section name (`\\\"Acceptance Criteria\\\"`) for self-fetch via `backlog_view`.\\n\\n**Other ad-hoc `dh:task-worker` dispatch sites found during this scan (not named in the item's original report \u2014 new to Impact Radius, scope decision needed):**\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/groom/error.md:18` \u2014 `Agent(subagent_type=\\\"dh:task-worker\\\", prompt=\\\"...\\\")` with inline content, same pattern as close/start.md.\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/groom/groom-drift.md:36,65` \u2014 two more `dh:task-worker` (haiku) spawns with prompt-embedded content.\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/quick/start.md:110,179` \u2014 dispatches a `task-worker` agent by name; not yet checked for pasted-content vs. self-fetch, flagged for audit.\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/work/post-planning.md:14` \u2014 documents dispatching `dh:task-worker` per task; not yet checked.\\n\\n**Explicitly out of scope per RT-ICA condition 7 (confirmed, not further audited here):** `plugins/development-harness/skills/add-new-feature/SKILL.md` and `plugins/development-harness/skills/implement-feature/SKILL.md` also construct dynamic dispatch prompts (plan-validator, contract-verification, feature-verifier, codebase-analyzer) but were not audited for this item \u2014 a broader fix (e.g. a shared-skill rule) will affect them even without direct edits, so any change to `subagent-contract` should be spot-checked against at least one of these two files before merging.\\n\\n**Identity-mismatch tangent (RT-ICA condition 8, scoping call not yet made):** `plugins/development-harness/agents/task-worker.md` describes a SAM-task-plan executor (reads via `sam_task`, not ad-hoc prompts), but close/start.md, groom/error.md, and groom-drift.md all dispatch it in an ad-hoc prompt mode that doesn't match its own Identity section. Recommend treating this as a separate follow-up item rather than folding it into this fix \u2014 flagging here so grooming doesn't silently drop it.\\n\\n**No production code, tests, or CI paths affected** \u2014 every affected file is agent/skill markdown documentation (prompt contracts), not executable code.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Session observation \u2014 development-harness groom workflow for #3169\\n- **Priority**: P2\\n- **Added**: 2026-08-28\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"5247f223728436050897e8e5dc2a5d6257485a6d09ae2481b99eeef5f32549ad\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABRQo0Dw\"}"}